## Why This Is Asked
Explores runtime extensibility using the Decorator and Registry patterns in a practical CLI context.

## Key Concepts
- **Registry pattern**: Centralized storage for cross-cutting concerns
- **Decorator pattern**: Wraps commands in layers for logging, retry, and metrics
- **Per-command isolation**: Faults in one decorator don't cascade to others
- **Runtime composition**: Decorator chains are assembled at execution time, not compile time

## Code Example
```typescript
interface Command {
  execute(ctx: any): Promise<void>;
}

interface Decorator extends Command {
  setNext(cmd: Command): Command;
}

class DecoratorRegistry {
  private decorators: Decorator[] = [];

  register(decorator: Decorator) {
    this.decorators.push(decorator);
  }

  compose(cmd: Command): Command {
    return this.decorators.reduceRight(
      (inner, decorator) => decorator.setNext(inner),
      cmd
    );
  }
}

const registry = new DecoratorRegistry();

registry.register({
  setNext(cmd) {
    return {
      execute: async (ctx) => {
        console.log('Logging:', ctx);
        await cmd.execute(ctx);
      },
    };
  },
} as Decorator);

class CommandFactory {
  private commands = new Map<string, Command>();

  register(name: string, cmd: Command) {
    this.commands.set(name, registry.compose(cmd));
  }

  execute(name: string, ctx: any) {
    return this.commands.get(name)!.execute(ctx);
  }
}

const factory = new CommandFactory();
factory.register('deploy', {
  execute: async (ctx) => console.log('Deploying:', ctx),
});

factory.execute('deploy', { env: 'prod' });
```

## Key Takeaway
Adding a new cross-cutting concern requires only registering a new decorator—no modifications to existing commands or the factory.

## Why This Is Asked
This question explores designing a runtime-extensible policy engine supporting hot-pluggable evaluators, multi-tenancy, and auditable decisions—realistic requirements for large-scale data platforms.

## Key Concepts
- Runtime registries and factories for pluggable evaluators
- Policy pipelines with per-tenant and per-resource scoping
- Chain of Responsibility pattern for sequential evaluation with short-circuiting
- Decision caching with invalidation on policy changes
- Precedence rules: explicit Deny overrides Allow; evaluators run in registered order unless overridden per resource

## Code Example
```typescript
interface EvaluationContext {
  tenant: string
  user: string
  resource: string
  action: string
  headers?: Record<string, string>
}

type Decision = 'Allow' | 'Deny'

interface EvaluationResult {
  decision: Decision
  evaluator: string
  reason?: string
}

interface IPolicyEvaluator {
  name: string
  evaluate(ctx: EvaluationContext): Promise<EvaluationResult>
}

class PolicyRegistry {
  private evaluators = new Map<string, IPolicyEvaluator>()

  register(evaluator: IPolicyEvaluator): void {
    this.evaluators.set(evaluator.name, evaluator)
  }

  get(name: string): IPolicyEvaluator | undefined {
    return this.evaluators.get(name)
  }

  getAll(): IPolicyEvaluator[] {
    return Array.from(this.evaluators.values())
  }
}

class PolicyPipeline {
  constructor(
    private registry: PolicyRegistry,
    private pipelineConfig: Map<string, string[]>
  ) {}

  async evaluate(ctx: EvaluationContext): Promise<EvaluationResult> {
    const evaluatorNames = this.pipelineConfig.get(ctx.resource) 
      ?? this.pipelineConfig.get('default') 
      ?? ['TenantRule', 'IPAllowlist', 'TimeWindow', 'DeviceTrust']

    for (const name of evaluatorNames) {
      const evaluator = this.registry.get(name)
      if (!evaluator) continue

      const result = await evaluator.evaluate(ctx)
      if (result.decision === 'Deny') {
        return result
      }
    }

    return { decision: 'Allow', evaluator: 'pipeline' }
  }
}

// Usage
const registry = new PolicyRegistry()
registry.register({ name: 'TenantRule', evaluate: async (ctx) => 
  ctx.tenant ? { decision: 'Allow', evaluator: 'TenantRule' } 
             : { decision: 'Deny', evaluator: 'TenantRule', reason: 'Missing tenant' }
})

const pipeline = new PolicyPipeline(registry, new Map([['default', ['TenantRule']]]))
const result = await pipeline.evaluate({ tenant: 'acme', user: 'alice', resource: 'file', action: 'read' })
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design extensible components with runtime plug-ins, and to coordinate parallel I/O with clear error reporting.

## Key Concepts
- Strategy pattern: pluggable channels
- Registry for runtime extensibility
- Promise.all or allSettled for parallel delivery
- Uniform per-channel error shape and per-channel isolation

## Code Example
```typescript
export interface Channel { send(msg: string, meta?: any): Promise<void> }
export type ChannelFactory = () => Channel
export class ChannelRegistry {
  private map = new Map<string, ChannelFactory>()
  register(name: string, factory: ChannelFactory) { this.map.set(name, factory) }
  create(name: string): Channel { const f = this.map.get(name); if (!f) throw new Error(`Unknown channel: ${name}`); return f() }
}

export class Notifier {
  constructor(private registry: ChannelRegistry) {}
  async notify(msg: string, channels: string[]) {
    const tasks = channels.map(n => {
      try { return this.registry.create(n).send(msg) } catch (e) { return Promise.reject(e) }
    })
    const results = await Promise.allSettled(tasks)
    return results.map((r, idx) => ({ channel: channels[idx], ok: r.status === 'fulfilled', error: r.status === 'rejected' ? (r.reason as Error).message : undefined }))
  }
}
```

