## Why This Is Asked
Tests runtime extensibility, deterministic composition, and thread-safety in a real fraud-detection context.

## Key Concepts
- Strategy pattern for individual rules
- Registry for dynamic, ordered plugins
- Thread-safety and immutability for runtime changes
- Deterministic evaluation order

## Code Example
```javascript
export interface FraudRule {
  priority: number
  compute(transaction: Transaction): number
}

export class RuleRegistry {
  private rules: FraudRule[] = []
  register(r: FraudRule) { this.rules.push(r); this.rules.sort((a,b)=>a.priority-b.priority) }
  evaluate(tx: Transaction): number {
    let score = 0
    for (const r of this.rules) score += r.compute(tx)
    return score
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle rule validity and versioning when a rule changes?
- Discuss testing strategies for rule interactions and order sensitivity.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply the Strategy pattern for runtime configurability, plus a registry to add new policies without touching cache internals.

## Key Concepts

- Strategy pattern
- Registry
- Runtime configurability
- Testability

## Code Example

```javascript
interface CachePolicy {
  onHit(key: string, value: any): void;
  evict(cache: Map<string, any>, capacity: number): string | null;
}
class Cache {
  private policy: CachePolicy;
  constructor(private store: Map<string, any>, registry: PolicyRegistry) {
    this.policy = registry.get('LRU');
  }
  access(key: string, value?: any) {
    if (value !== undefined) this.store.set(key, value);
    this.policy.onHit(key, this.store);
    if (this.store.size > 100) {
      const evicted = this.policy.evict(this.store, 100);
      if (evicted) this.store.delete(evicted);
    }
  }
}
class PolicyRegistry {
  private factories: Map<string, () => CachePolicy> = new Map();
  register(name: string, f: () => CachePolicy) { this.factories.set(name, f); }
  get(name: string): CachePolicy { return this.factories.get(name)!(); }
}
```

